Feature: Do not treat punctuation in a generated marker as part of its command
  This feature checks the command that generatedMarkerOf returns for a large generated file whose
  marker reads Code generated by a command and DO NOT EDIT.

  Scenario: A period before DO NOT EDIT is not part of the command
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 1 and reads "// Code generated by go run gen.go.  DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the command is "go run gen.go" on line 1

  Scenario: Punctuation inside the command is kept
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 1 and reads "// Code generated by go run gen.go -version=1.5.  DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the command is "go run gen.go -version=1.5" on line 1

  Scenario: Dots inside a command with no sentence period are kept
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 1 and reads "# Code generated by sh gen.sh --out=a.b.c DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the command is "sh gen.sh --out=a.b.c" on line 1

  Scenario: A semicolon before DO NOT EDIT is still dropped
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 5 and reads "# Code generated by sh gen/table.sh; DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the command is "sh gen/table.sh" on line 5

  Scenario: A period and a single space before DO NOT EDIT is still dropped
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 1 and reads "// Code generated by go run gen.go. DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the command is "go run gen.go" on line 1

  Scenario: A marker without punctuation keeps its whole command
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 1 and reads "// Code generated by go run gen.go DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the command is "go run gen.go" on line 1

  Scenario: A marker after line 5 is not recognised
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 6 and reads "# Code generated by sh gen/table.sh; DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the file is not recognised as generated

  Scenario: A marker with an empty command is not recognised
    Given review-generated-marker-command-parsing: a generated file over 6000 bytes whose marker is on line 1 and reads "# Code generated by  ; DO NOT EDIT."
    When review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the generated marker of that file is parsed
    Then review-generated-marker-command-parsing: the file is not recognised as generated
